    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 190</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_190/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 19 Feb 2025 02:08:43 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_190/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;What are the scenarios for memory leaks?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Long-lived objects holding references to short-lived objects: For example, objects referenced by global variables.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Unclosed Goroutines: If a Goroutine starts but fails to terminate normally due to some reason (e.g., deadlock, infinite loop, channel blocking), it will continue to occupy memory.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Improper use of the &lt;code&gt;time&lt;/code&gt; package.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Improper string operations.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Unclosed resources like files and network connections.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Improper CGO memory management: If your Go code uses CGO and allocates memory in C code, you need to manually free that memory. Otherwise, it will lead to a memory leak.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;</description>
    </item>
